        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Who this handbook is for",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-who-this-handbook-is-for.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## Who this handbook is for\n\nThis handbook has been developed for countries, organizations and teams seeking to plan and implement digital solutions for reporting substandard and falsified medical products by health care professionals and aims to provide guidance to the various target audiences across the different implementation phases and areas, as outlined in Table 1.\n\n**Table 1. Intended audience for the handbook**\n\n| Handbook section – phases | Illustrative target organizations | Illustrative target user teams and roles | Purpose of the section |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. Assess the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines the target audience for the handbook and presents Table 1 mapping each of the seven handbook chapters/phases to illustrative target organizations, target user teams and roles, and section purpose.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How to use this handbook",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-how-to-use-this-handbook.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## How to use this handbook\n\nThis handbook provides guidance to countries on how to adopt digital solutions for reporting of substandard and falsified medical products. It outlines various steps to ensure a comprehensive approach that aligns with existing digital health guidance and other intersecting strategies and architectures.\n\nThis handbook can be a useful resource in the following scenarios:\n\n- When moving from a paper-only based reporting system to a digital reporting system.\n- When moving to a unified digital solution and approach, e.g. when there are some groups of health care profess",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Outlines four implementation scenarios for using the handbook, prerequisites around national digital health and supply chain strategies, alignment with the WHO Digital implementation investment guide (DIIG) across seven enterprise phases (Fig. 1), and alignment with WHO SMART Guidelines knowledge layers L1–L5 (Fig. 2).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "How this handbook was developed",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-how-this-handbook-was-developed.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## How this handbook was developed\n\nThis handbook draws on the knowledge of health care professionals and NMRAs globally and has collated the lessons learned from their experiences. It builds on insights from a pilot phase, benchmarking exercises with national authorities, consultations with Member States on substandard and falsified medical product reporting barriers, public feedback on a drafted version of the handbook and the latest round of consultations for this final product. Practical examples and templates were also informed by real-world implementation of substandard and falsified med",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the development of the handbook through global consultations with health care professionals, NMRAs, and Member States, pilot implementations in Indonesia and the United Republic of Tanzania, and alignment with the Principles for Digital Development, DIIG, and the WHO/ITU National eHealth strategy toolkit.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Assess the current state and enabling environment",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-1-assess-the-current-state-and-enabling-environm.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "# 1. Assess the current state and enabling environment\n\nA comprehensive assessment of the current state and the enabling environment is necessary before implementing a digital reporting system for substandard and falsified medical products. This chapter outlines the areas that should be analysed and the factors to consider. The inputs needed to inform this step and the anticipated outputs of this phase are listed below.\n\n| Inputs | Outputs | Resources (see page 50) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| • National health strategy, digital health strategy and architecture blueprint<br>• National health, data",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Chapter 1 opener outlining the inputs, outputs (situational analysis report and stakeholder matrix), and reference resources required to assess the current state and enabling environment before implementing a digital reporting system.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Analyse key stakeholders",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-11-analyse-key-stakeholders.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## 1.1 Analyse key stakeholders\n\nTo implement an effective digital substandard and falsified reporting solution, it is essential to identify and analyse stakeholders. Stakeholders include government officials, health care providers, funding organizations, the medical product industry, technology vendors and implementers, civil society and other partners. The analysis should outline their roles in substandard and falsified medical products, pharmacovigilance and digital health. It should help mobilize support and engage key players by examining responsibilities, information flows and power dyna",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Guides stakeholder identification and analysis across government, health care providers, funders, industry, implementers, and civil society, and presents Table 2 with sample stakeholder analysis questions across six dimensions (roles and responsibilities, information flows, influence and power, interests and expectations, engagement and collaboration, and capacity and resources).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Assess the digital readiness",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-12-assess-the-digital-readiness.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## 1.2 Assess the digital readiness\n\nDifferent levels of preparedness for deploying digital technologies will influence the design, implementation and adoption of reporting apps. As one of the first steps, a digital readiness assessment should be conducted to ensure solutions are tailored to the existing infrastructure, capacity and context. The outputs from the assessment should inform the necessary resources, enabling environment, budget and funding support for planning, implementation and operationalization of the substandard and falsified reporting system.\n\nThe following areas should be re",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Outlines the six areas of a country digital readiness assessment (national digital health strategy, enabling policy and regulatory environment, governance framework, human resources, technology infrastructure, and security and privacy protection) and presents Table 3 on assessing network coverage, hardware/software availability, infrastructure, security, privacy, and data ownership.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Assess current substandard and falsified reporting practices",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-13-assess-current-substandard-and-falsified-repo.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## 1.3 Assess current substandard and falsified reporting practices\n\n**Current practices.** Conduct an analysis of current practices, challenges and barriers faced in reporting substandard and falsified medical products. The outputs of the analysis should be used to develop optimal business processes and workflows within the application. It is important to examine substandard and falsified reporting procedures and their challenges, alongside pharmacovigilance procedures to identify potential opportunities for synergy. The integration of these processes can enhance their mutual effectiveness in",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Provides guidance on evaluating current substandard and falsified reporting practices, data collection forms, digital tools, and reporting barriers, presenting Table 4 (assessing pharmacovigilance and SF reporting practices and data usage), Table 5 (common challenges and barriers across seven categories with potential solutions, including anonymous reporting considerations), and the Chapter 1 checklist.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Establish a shared understanding and governance framework",
      "pageStart": 26,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-2-establish-a-shared-understanding-and-governanc.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "# 2. Establish a shared understanding and governance framework\n\nImplementing digital reporting systems requires more than just technology. It calls for a clear, strategic and participatory approach that aligns regulatory policy, governance and leadership. This section explores how countries can establish a shared understanding of the challenges and opportunities surrounding substandard and falsified medical products and create a strategic plan to ensure sustainable, effective adoption of digital health tools.\n\n| Inputs | Outputs | Resources (see page 50) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| • Current state",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Chapter 2 opener summarizing the inputs, outputs (new/amended regulatory policies, governance structures, and strategic goals), and resources for establishing a shared understanding and governance framework.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Review regulatory policies for substandard and falsified medical products reporting",
      "pageStart": 27,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-21-review-regulatory-policies-for-substandard-an.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## 2.1 Review regulatory policies for substandard and falsified medical products reporting\n\nThe regulatory framework is the backbone for any substandard and falsified medical products reporting system, shaping data flow, privacy, liability and enforcement mechanisms. Before implementing a digital reporting system, countries should evaluate existing regulatory policies, identify gaps and determine whether new regulations or legislative amendments are necessary to support the implementation, operationalization and adoption.\n\n- **Pharmacovigilance and quality surveillance integration.** Substanda",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Guides the review of national regulatory frameworks supporting digital reporting of substandard and falsified medical products, covering pharmacovigilance and quality surveillance integration, mandatory and voluntary reporting obligations, and enforcement mechanisms.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Align policy with stakeholders",
      "pageStart": 28,
      "pageEnd": 28,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-22-align-policy-with-stakeholders.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## 2.2 Align policy with stakeholders\n\nTackling substandard and falsified medical products requires more than a single institution. Effective policy depends on multisectoral collaboration across:\n\n- **Law enforcement and customs.** Align policies to enable swift data sharing with customs officers and law enforcement, who often spearhead investigations and border control.\n- **Health financing and procurement agencies.** Support policy coordination with procurement bodies and insurance systems to detect falsified products within supply chains or reimbursement claims.\n- **Innovation and ICT minis",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes multisectoral policy alignment across law enforcement and customs, health financing and procurement agencies, innovation and ICT ministries, and regional and global regulatory authorities.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Establish governance and leadership",
      "pageStart": 28,
      "pageEnd": 28,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-23-establish-governance-and-leadership.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## 2.3 Establish governance and leadership\n\nEstablish a robust governance structure for coordinating the multiple stakeholders involved in substandard and falsified medical product reporting. Define clear roles and responsibilities to avoid duplication of effort, enhance transparency and create a stable platform for decision-making.\n\nA governance structure often includes:\n\n- **National steering committee.** Led by the ministry of health or NMRA, this committee provides strategic oversight, ensures alignment with national health goals and mobilizes resources. The national steering committee dri",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines a three-tier governance structure (national steering committee, technical working group, and advisory council) and key leadership roles (ministry of health/NMRA, project champion, and digital health coordinator/project manager).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "antigravity",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240120747-eng/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Set the strategic goals",
      "pageStart": 28,
      "pageEnd": 29,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-24-set-the-strategic-goals.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## 2.4 Set the strategic goals\n\nDefine strategic goals for the substandard and falsified reporting application so that it is built to appropriately address prioritized needs. These goals should be:\n\n- **Specific.** For instance, reduce the average detection-to-notification time for suspected substandard and falsified medical products from 14 days to 2 days.\n- **Measurable.** Use indicators like the number of detected substandard and falsified incidents, time to regulatory action, or user engagement metrics in the digital reporting platform.\n- **Achievable.** Align goals with available resource",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
